/** recipe/DRAW.md */
export const DRAW = "# Draw the system: the first drawing\n\nYou are drawing a project's system: a set of sheets a person can zoom through, where every part and every line names\nthe files it rests on. The drawing is a folder of YAML files, `.plannotator/system/`, committed to the project's repo.\nThe person sees it in the Plannotator Inbox as you write it. The format is at the end of these instructions, under\n\"System format 1\".\n\nThe facts of this job (the project, the folder to work in, what to read) are listed above these instructions.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Work only in the drawing's worktree, the folder named above. Never edit, switch or commit in the person's own\n  checkout. Every command below runs in the worktree, and every check reads the worktree's own\n  `.plannotator/system/`, never another copy of the drawing.\n- Draw what the code says today, never what you remember or what a README or a marketing page claims. Every part,\n  line and label must trace to a file you read. If you cannot point to a file for a claim, leave the claim out.\n- Read only. Other repos are cloned read only into a temporary folder. Never push to the default branch; you push one\n  branch, `plannotator/system`, and open one pull request.\n- Write to the person in the Inbox only where these steps say so: one message when you start reading and one when\n  you are done. Send them with your Plannotator Inbox tool's `send_message`, into this thread. If you have no Inbox\n  tool, run `plannotator inbox call send_message '{\"body\": \"<text>\"}'` in a shell.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Get the worktree ready.** If the worktree folder does not exist, make it from the project's folder:\n   `git -C <project folder> fetch origin` and then\n   `git -C <project folder> worktree add -b plannotator/system <worktree> origin/<default branch>`.\n   Find the default branch with `git -C <project folder> symbolic-ref --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD`. If no\n   worktree folder was named above, make one in a new empty folder of your own outside the project. Then work only\n   there. Note the commit you are drawing from: `git -C <worktree> rev-parse HEAD`.\n\n2. **Read the code.** Start with how the project is built and started: the package or build files, the entry points,\n   the servers, workers and processes, the stores (databases, files, queues, caches), the routes and commands, the\n   configuration and its switches (feature flags, environment defaults). For each other repo named above, clone it\n   read only (`git clone --filter=blob:none <url> <a temporary folder>`) and note its commit with\n   `git rev-parse HEAD`. Read the doc sites named above for names and intent, but draw only what the code confirms.\n   - A switch that is off by default is drawn with \"off\" in its part's `sub` or its band's `means`, and the file the\n     switch lives in is in the part's `code:`. Read the default; do not guess it.\n   - Retired, removed or dead code is not drawn.\n   - A part is a runtime piece (a process, server, worker, window, store, agent host, outside service) or a module\n     someone working on the code must be able to find. A helper module is not a part: cite it in the `code:` of the\n     part that runs it.\n   - People, outside vendors, the operating system and products built in other repos are outside parts: no `code:`,\n     in a band of their own (for example \"Outside\" or \"Who uses it\"), never mixed into a band of the project's own\n     pieces.\n\n3. **Write `system.yaml` first.** In `<worktree>/.plannotator/system/system.yaml` write `format: 1`, `system` (the\n   project's name), `root: C-001`, `sources` (this repo as `self` with its `repo` and `at`, the commit from step 1;\n   each other repo under a short alias with its own `at`; the doc sites under `docs`), `scopes`, and `wanted:` with\n   EVERY sheet you plan, the landing `C-001` first. Add the parts and lines you know so far. The Inbox counts the\n   person's progress from `wanted:`. Then send your first message: one or two sentences on what you are reading and\n   the sheets you plan.\n\n4. **Draw the landing, `sheets/C-001.yaml`.** A conceptual sheet of scope `root`: who uses the project, what runs\n   where, and the main lines between them, in three or four bands. A newcomer must understand it in one minute. Keep\n   words short. A large piece with its own insides is a part with `child:` that opens a scope: its insides go on that\n   scope's sheets, not on the landing.\n\n5. **Draw the deeper sheets.** For each scope, a logical sheet (`L-...`) of what runs inside it. Then the flows\n   (`F-...`) someone working on the code would follow: one request or one job, hop by hop, with the file and function\n   in the hop's words or payload. Add an algorithm (`A-...`), deployment (`D-...`) or landscape (`T-...`) sheet only\n   when it answers a real question. Fewer good sheets beat many thin ones. A sheet past about 30 parts, or with lines\n   that tangle, moves a group of parts into a window: a part with `child:`, a new scope and a new sheet. As each\n   sheet's file is written and loads clean, take its code out of `wanted:`.\n\n6. **Check, and fix until clean.** Run `plannotator system check --root <worktree>`. It loads the drawing and checks\n   that every cited path is in the repo at the commit in `sources.repos.self.at`. For another repo add\n   `--repo <alias>=<its temporary folder>`. It prints each error with its file and field, and each missing path with\n   the field that cites it (`parts.<id>.code`, or `flows.<id>.code.from` or `.to` for a line). Fix and run it again until\n   it prints no errors, no warnings and no missing paths. Then read your sheets once more against the code: every\n   claim must be true at the commit you drew from.\n\n7. **Hand it over.** In the worktree, commit only `.plannotator/system/`:\n   `git -C <worktree> add .plannotator/system` and\n   `git -C <worktree> commit -m \"System: first drawing of <project>\"`. Push the branch:\n   `git -C <worktree> push -u origin plannotator/system`. Open one pull request with\n   `gh pr create --base <default branch> --head plannotator/system --title \"System: first drawing of <project>\"`\n   and a body that lists the sheets, one line each. If the push or `gh` fails (no rights, no `gh`), say so in your\n   last message; the drawing stays in the worktree and the Inbox still shows it.\n\n8. **Send the last message**: the sheets you drew, one line each; the pull request's link; and the sentence \"I will\n   redraw it when main moves.\" Then stop.\n";

/** recipe/UPDATE.md */
export const UPDATE = "# Redraw the system: bring the drawing up to date\n\nThe project's main branch moved, and the files it changed may have moved the shape of the system. You bring the\ndrawing in `.plannotator/system/` up to date with the code at the new commit. Nobody is watching: work alone, and\nstop when the drawing is right or when you decide it does not change. The format is at the end of these\ninstructions, under \"System format 1\".\n\nThe facts of this job are listed above these instructions: the worktree to work in, the range of commits that\nlanded, the merged pull requests, why the check woke you (the changed files and their reasons), the parts and lines\nthat cite a changed file, and the file to write your summary in.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Work only in the worktree named above. It is already on the redraw branch at the new commit. Read the code there.\n- Edit only files under `.plannotator/system/`, plus the summary file named above. Change no other file.\n- Do not commit, push, open a pull request or send a message. The scheduled check does all of that after you stop,\n  and it refuses the redraw if any other file changed.\n- Draw what the code says at the new commit, never what a commit message or a pull request's text claims.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Read what changed.** Run `git -C <worktree> diff --name-status -M <from> <to>` and\n   `git -C <worktree> log --oneline <from>..<to>`. Read the diff of each file named in \"why you were woken\"\n   (`git -C <worktree> diff <from> <to> -- <path>`). Tests, docs and lockfiles do not count. Look for new, deleted\n   and renamed files, and for new processes, stores, hosts, routes, commands, connections and switches.\n\n2. **Decide whether the shape moved.** Find the parts and lines that cite each changed file:\n   `grep -n \"<path>\" <worktree>/.plannotator/system/system.yaml`. If every changed file is already named and the\n   change adds no process, store, host, route family, connection or switch, and removes none, the drawing does not\n   change. Then change nothing at all, not even `at`: write \"No change:\" and one sentence of why in the summary file,\n   and stop.\n\n3. **Edit `system.yaml` to the code at the new commit.**\n   - A new runtime piece (a process, server, worker, window, store, agent host, outside service) is a new part:\n     `in:`, `name`, `kind`, `role` (one sentence a newcomer can read), and `code:` with its files.\n   - A new call between two parts is a new line: `from`, `to`, `says`, and `code:` where a file shows it. If it\n     crosses into a window, name the part inside with `lands:` (or `leaves:` for the start).\n   - A switch: read its default in the code. Off by default is drawn with \"off\" in the part's `sub` or its band's\n     `means`, and the file the switch lives in is in `code:`.\n   - Removed code: delete its part and its lines from `system.yaml`, and take its id out of every sheet (band rows,\n     `show`, `quiet`, `says`, `notes`, `callouts`, flow `lanes` and `hops`). Never draw removed code as live.\n   - A moved or renamed file: change its path in every `code:` map that cites it.\n\n4. **Place each new part and line on a sheet.** A new part goes in one band `row` on the logical sheet of its scope;\n   a new line goes in that sheet's `show`. If the change alters a walk, change the hops of its flow sheet. If a sheet\n   passes about 30 parts or its lines tangle, move a group into a window (a part with `child:`, a new scope and a new\n   sheet).\n\n5. **Keep the change, when a stored review is named above.** A stored review is the change a reviewer drew while the\n   pull request was open. Use it as a head start for steps 3 and 4. You may also keep it in the repo as\n   `.plannotator/system/revisions/PR-<n>.yaml` in the merged form: each added part and line written `id: ~` (it is\n   now in `system.yaml`, with the same id), no `place:`, `merged:` set to the merge commit, and the rest as the review\n   wrote it, corrected to what landed.\n\n6. **Move `at`.** Set `sources.repos.self.at` in `system.yaml` to the new commit named above, in quotes.\n\n7. **Check, and fix until clean.** Run `plannotator system check --root <worktree>`. Fix every error, warning and\n   missing path it prints (a missing path names the field that cites it, `parts.<id>.code` or\n   `flows.<id>.code.from` or `.to`), and run it again until it prints none.\n\n8. **Write the summary file** named above: one sentence per sheet you changed, in plain words for the person, for\n   example \"On the Desktop app sheet, stable now shows a corner, not a header.\" Then stop.\n";

/** recipe/REVIEW.md */
export const REVIEW = "# Draw a pull request's change on the system\n\nA pull request is open and you are reviewing it. The project has a drawing of its system in `.plannotator/system/`\non its default branch. You draw how this pull request changes that drawing, so the person sees the change in place:\nadded parts green, changed parts amber, removed parts grey. You write one revision file and hand it to Plannotator.\nYou never commit it and never push it to the pull request's branch. The format is at the end of these instructions,\nunder \"System format 1\"; a revision is under \"revisions/<id>.yaml\".\n\nYou need four facts, from the review you are doing: the project's folder, the pull request (`host/owner/repo#n`),\nits head commit and its base branch.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Ask the check first.** Find the merge base: `git -C <project folder> merge-base origin/<base> <head>`. Run\n   `plannotator system gate --project <project folder> --from <merge base> --to <head>`, with the merge base as the\n   sha `merge-base` printed: the gate diffs exactly the two commits it is given and finds no merge base itself. It\n   reads the drawing on disk in the project folder, whichever branch is checked out there, so run it where the base\n   branch is checked out: `touched` then names the parts and lines of the drawing the change lands on. It prints JSON: `touched`\n   (the parts and lines that cite a changed file), `wake` and `why`. If `wake` is false, stop here: the change is\n   drawn from `touched` alone, with no revision from you.\n\n2. **Read the drawing as committed** on the base branch:\n   `git -C <project folder> show origin/<base>:.plannotator/system/system.yaml`, and each sheet the touched parts are\n   on (`git -C <project folder> show origin/<base>:.plannotator/system/sheets/<CODE>.yaml`). Note the ids of the\n   parts, lines and bands you will name.\n\n3. **Read the change.** `git -C <project folder> diff --name-status -M <merge base> <head>` and\n   `git -C <project folder> diff --numstat <merge base> <head>`, then the diff of each file in `why`. Tests, docs and\n   lockfiles do not count. Draw what the code does at the head commit, never what the pull request's text claims.\n\n4. **Write the revision in the open form.** The drawing does not have the pull request's new parts yet, so:\n   - `revision: PR-<n>`, `scope` (the scope the change lives in; `root` when it spans scopes), `title` (what the\n     change does, in a few words), `pr: <n>`, `repo: <host/owner/repo>`, `date` (today, quoted). No `merged:`.\n   - `add.parts` and `add.flows`: each new part and line as a full entry, with `code:`. `place`: for each new part,\n     the sheet and band it goes in (`{ <CODE>: { <id>: { band: <band id>, after: <part id>, span: <1 to 12> } } }`).\n   - `change`: one sentence per changed part or line, naming what it now does.\n   - `remove`: each removed part or line, with one sentence; a removed line keeps its old ends\n     (`flow: { from, to, says }`) so Before can draw it.\n   - `lines`: per changed or added part, its files and counts from `--numstat`, for example\n     `\"worker.ts and 2 more · +120 −31\"`.\n   - `steps`: the one sequence the pull request changes, about eight hops on its own `lanes`, each hop marked\n     `is: added` or `is: changed` when it is new or different.\n\n5. **Hand it to Plannotator.** Pipe the YAML to\n   `plannotator system revision --project <project folder> --pr <host/owner/repo#n> --head <head> -`. It loads the\n   revision against the drawing and refuses one that does not load, naming the field. Fix the field and send it\n   again until it is accepted. Do not commit the file anywhere.\n";

/** recipe/FORMAT.md */
export const FORMAT = "# System format 1: what to write\n\nA drawing is one folder, `.plannotator/system/`, in the project's repo:\n\n```\n.plannotator/system/\n  system.yaml            required: every part and line, each fact once\n  sheets/<CODE>.yaml     one file per sheet; the file is named after its code\n  revisions/<id>.yaml    optional: the change of one merged pull request (PR-<n>.yaml)\n```\n\nNothing in a drawing is a coordinate. A sheet says where a part belongs (its band, its order in the band and its\nspan), never where it sits. The loader refuses `x`, `y`, `w`, `h`, `lx`, `ly`, `fx`, `fy`, `tx`, `ty`, `via`, `pts`\nand `px`, refuses any key it does not know, and checks every id a file names. An unquoted comma inside `{ }` splits\none text into two keys, so quote any text that has a comma, a colon or a `#` in it.\n\n## system.yaml\n\n```yaml\nformat: 1                           # always 1\nsystem: Acme                        # the display name\nroot: C-001                         # the landing sheet's code\nsources:                            # what you read\n  repos:\n    self: { repo: github.com/acme/acme, at: \"<full commit sha>\" }   # this repo; at: the commit you drew from, quoted\n    api: { repo: github.com/acme/api, at: \"<full commit sha>\" }     # another repo, under an alias you choose\n  docs: [https://docs.example.com]\nwatch: [deploy/*.toml]              # optional: globs (** crosses folders, * and ? stay in one) of files whose edit changes the shape\nscopes: { root: Acme, app: The app } # a scope is what a window opens into; root is the landing\nwanted:                             # sheets planned and not drawn yet; take each out once its file is written\n  - { code: A-201, kind: algorithm, question: \"How an order is priced, step by step.\" }\nparts:\n  person: { name: You, kind: people, role: Signs in and orders. }\n  app:\n    name: The app                   # what a newcomer calls it\n    kind: web app                   # one or two words\n    role: Serves every page and the API.    # one sentence a newcomer can read\n    sub: Node 22, one process       # optional: one short mono line under the name\n    marks: [ts]                     # optional: logo names; an unknown name draws as a small tag\n    child: app                      # optional: this part opens the scope `app` (drawn as a window you dive into)\n    facts: { Port: \"8080 (server.ts)\" }    # optional: short label to value pairs\n    code:                           # file or folder path: what it does for this part\n      src/server.ts: the entry\n      api:src/client.ts: a path in the repo with alias api\n  web:\n    in: app                         # its scope; leave it out for root\n    name: Web server\n    kind: process\n    role: Answers every request.\n    code: { src/routes: every route }\n  worker: { in: app, name: Worker, kind: process, role: Takes orders and charges them., code: { src/worker.ts: the loop } }\n  db: { in: app, name: Postgres, kind: store, role: Holds accounts and orders., code: { src/db/pool.ts: the pool } }\n  cache: { in: app, name: Cache, kind: store, role: Keeps prices for an hour., sub: \"in memory, off in tests\", code: { src/cache.ts: the cache } }\nflows:\n  use:\n    from: person                    # one part, or a list of parts\n    to: app\n    says: opens a page              # the words on the line, a few words\n    carries: the session cookie     # optional: what travels\n    code: { to: src/routes/index.ts }      # optional: the file at either end\n    lands: web                      # when `to` opens a scope: the part inside that the line reaches\n    both: false                     # optional: arrowheads at both ends\n    moves: data                     # optional: data, people, money or energy\n  w_db: { from: worker, to: db, says: reads and writes orders, code: { from: src/worker.ts } }\n  w_cache: { from: worker, to: cache, says: looks up a price }\nstack:                              # optional: technology tiles for a landscape sheet; each is a part\n  t-node: { name: Node, mark: node, for: the server, used_by: [app] }\n```\n\n- `code:` is what keeps a drawing true. Every part and line that rests on code names its files, in two shapes: a\n  part's `code:` maps each path to what that file does for it (`code: { src/worker.ts: the loop }`); a line's\n  `code:` names the file at each end (`code: { from: src/worker.ts, to: src/db/pool.ts }`), either end optional. A path with no\n  prefix is in this repo; `<alias>:<path>` is in another repo named in `sources.repos`. A path is relative to its\n  repo's root: no leading `/`, no `..`. A cited folder covers every file in it. A part with no code (a person, an\n  outside vendor, a product built elsewhere) has no `code:`.\n- `leaves:` is `lands:` for the `from` end, when the line starts inside a window.\n- Every scope but `root` is opened by exactly one part (`child:`). A scope may sit inside another scope.\n- Ids are short lowercase words you choose (`app`, `db`, `w_db`). Never `__proto__`, `constructor` or `prototype`.\n\n## sheets/<CODE>.yaml\n\nA sheet answers one question about one scope. The code's letter is its kind's letter and the number is yours:\nC conceptual, L logical, D deployment, S service, F flow, A algorithm, T landscape. Required: `sheet`, `scope`,\n`kind`, `title`, and `bands` (or `lanes`, `steps` or `columns` for flow, algorithm and landscape sheets).\n\nThe landing is a sheet of scope `root`:\n\n```yaml\nsheet: C-001\nscope: root\nkind: conceptual\ntitle: Acme\nquestion: What Acme is, who uses it, and where it runs.\nbands:\n  - { id: people, label: Who uses it, tone: sand, row: [person] }\n  - { id: hosted, label: Hosted, tone: mist, row: [app] }\nshow: [use]\n```\n\nA logical sheet shows what is inside one window:\n\n```yaml\nsheet: L-101\nscope: app\nkind: logical\ntitle: The app\nquestion: What runs inside the app and where it keeps its data.\nbands:                              # rows, top to bottom\n  - id: doors\n    label: Ways in                  # sentence case, never capitals\n    means: started by a request     # optional: a short line under the label\n    tone: sand                      # sand, mist, stone or sage\n    row: [web 6, worker 6]          # part ids left to right; \"id N\" spans N of 12 columns\n  - id: data\n    label: On disk\n    tone: stone\n    row: [db 4, ~ 4, cache 4]       # \"~ N\" is a gap of N columns; [a, b, 4] stacks a and b in one cell\n    split: { before: cache, left: kept, right: \"thrown away on restart\" }\nshow: [w_db, w_cache]               # the lines drawn on this sheet, or `all`\nquiet: [w_cache]                    # optional: lines whose words show only on hover\nsays: { w_db: writes orders }       # optional: other words for a line on this sheet only\nnotes:                              # optional: lettered notes on a part, a line or a hop\n  a: { on: db, text: \"The worker is the only writer.\" }\ncallouts: []                        # optional: { to: <part>, side, title, text }\ngroups: []                          # optional: { label, marks, around: [band ids] }\nbrace: { over: [doors], text: Node }   # optional: a bracket over bands\nleft: []                            # optional side columns: { label, means, tone, beside: <band id>, stack: [part ids] }\nright: []\n```\n\nA flow sheet walks one request hop by hop:\n\n```yaml\nsheet: F-101\nscope: app\nkind: flow\ntitle: Sign in\nquestion: How a sign-in travels, hop by hop.\ntime: in order\nlanes: [person, web, db]            # parts, left to right\nplaces:                             # optional: bands over lanes\n  - { label: Your browser, tone: mist, lanes: [person] }\nhops:\n  - { id: post, from: person, to: web, says: POST /login, flow: use, at: \"1\" }\n  - { id: look, from: web, to: db, says: find the account, at: \"2\", payload: { name: query, lines: [\"SELECT ... WHERE email = $1\"] } }\n  - { id: ok, from: web, to: person, reply: true, says: \"302 and a cookie\", at: \"3\" }\n  - { id: log, on: web, do: writes the audit line, at: \"4\" }\n```\n\nAn algorithm sheet (A) adds `endpoint`, `route`, `steps` (`id`, `do`, `code`, `if`, `no`, `yes_says`, `no_says`,\n`calls`, `flow`, `says`, `end`), `request` and `response`. A landscape sheet (T) adds `columns` (`label`, `means`,\n`tone`, `row` of stack ids, `says`).\n\n## revisions/<id>.yaml: one change\n\nOne schema serves a merged pull request kept in the repo and an open pull request's change kept outside it. The\nmerged form:\n\n```yaml\nrevision: PR-412                    # the file is revisions/PR-412.yaml\nscope: app                          # the scope the change lives in; root when it spans scopes\ntitle: The worker gets a price cache\npr: 412\nrepo: github.com/acme/acme\nmerged: 9c41e07                     # the merge commit\ndate: \"2026-11-03\"\nadd:\n  parts: { cache: ~ }               # `~`: the part is already in system.yaml; this PR added it\n  flows: { w_cache: ~ }\nchange:\n  worker: Looks a price up in the cache before it charges.      # one sentence per changed part or line\nremove:\n  w_poll: { says: \"The worker no longer polls.\", flow: { from: worker, to: db, says: polls } }   # a removed line keeps its old ends\nlines:\n  worker: \"worker.ts and 2 more · +120 −31\"   # the diff counts per part\nsteps:                              # optional: the one sequence the change makes, about eight hops\n  title: A price on the way to a charge\n  lanes: [worker, cache, db]\n  hops:\n    - { from: worker, to: cache, says: look up the price, is: added }\n    - { from: cache, to: worker, reply: true, says: the price, is: added }\n    - { from: worker, to: db, says: write the charge, is: changed }\n```\n\n`is` is `added` or `changed`; a hop with no `is` was there before.\n\nThe open form is for a pull request that is not merged yet, so the drawing does not have its new parts. Each added\npart or line is a full entry, and `place` says where each new part goes on each sheet:\n\n```yaml\nrevision: PR-415\nscope: app\ntitle: Orders get a queue\npr: 415\nrepo: github.com/acme/acme\nadd:\n  parts: { queue: { in: app, name: Queue, kind: store, role: Holds orders until the worker takes them., code: { src/queue.ts: the queue } } }\n  flows: { w_queue: { from: worker, to: queue, says: takes the next order } }\n  place: { L-101: { queue: { band: data, after: db, span: 4 } } }\nchange:\n  worker: Takes orders from the queue, not from the database.\nlines:\n  worker: \"worker.ts · +40 −12\"\n```\n\n## Words\n\n- Sentence case everywhere. No capitals for emphasis, no letterspaced labels.\n- No process words on a sheet: nothing like \"checked against\", \"drawn by\", \"curated\", \"sample\", \"TODO\".\n- No performance numbers (latency, rates, sizes of traffic) and no invented counts. A count goes on a sheet only when\n  one cited file gives it exactly; when in doubt, leave the count out.\n- Something that is configured off, retired or removed is not drawn as live. Off by default is drawn with \"off\" in\n  its `sub` or its band's `means`, and the file the switch lives in is in its `code:`.\n\n## Removed keys\n\nThe loader refuses these and says why: `sample`, `record`, `history`, `prs`, `issues`, `threads`, `charts`,\n`figures`, `rate`, and on a revision `state`, `author`, `age`, `workspace` and `by`.\n";
